SAT_CHUNK("rw/inferences/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.a700912adb","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-22","passage":"<p>Researchers studying the Pacific salmon found that fish raised in hatcheries and released into rivers return to spawn at lower rates than wild-born fish. One hypothesis holds that hatchery fish are genetically less suited to life in the wild. However, in a recent study, eggs from wild parents that were raised in a hatchery for their first year also returned at low rates, while eggs from hatchery parents raised in natural streams returned at rates similar to those of wild fish. These results suggest that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["salmon raised in hatcheries are genetically less suited to life in the wild than wild-born salmon are.","eggs from wild parents are more likely to survive in hatcheries than eggs from hatchery parents are.","the environment in which salmon spend their early lives may matter more for their later survival than their parents' origin does.","hatchery-raised salmon return to spawn at higher rates when their parents were also raised in hatcheries, since those salmon inherit traits suited to hatchery life."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Wild-parent eggs raised in hatcheries did poorly, and hatchery-parent eggs raised in streams did well. So early environment, not parentage, predicts survival. That undermines the genetic hypothesis. The study says nothing about higher return rates for fish whose parents were also hatchery-raised, and it doesn't compare egg survival inside hatcheries."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.ad40a73e85","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-23","passage":"<p>In the 1700s, many European porcelain makers tried to copy the fine white porcelain imported from China, but their products cracked in the kiln or came out gray. Chemist Johann Friedrich Böttger eventually succeeded in Meissen, Germany, in 1708, after experimenting with a white clay called kaolin from local deposits. Chinese potters had used kaolin for centuries, and European traders had described Chinese porcelain in detail but had not brought back samples of its raw materials. This suggests that the earlier European failures resulted at least partly from the fact that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["Böttger had learned about kaolin from the detailed descriptions provided by traders.","European makers were unable to build kilns that reached the high temperatures needed to keep porcelain from cracking or turning gray.","European makers lacked the key ingredient rather than simply the right techniques.","Chinese potters deliberately kept their methods secret from European traders."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Böttger succeeded once he used kaolin, the clay Chinese potters used, and traders hadn't brought back raw materials. So Europeans were missing an ingredient. The text never discusses kiln temperatures or Chinese secrecy. Traders described the finished porcelain, not its clay, so Böttger didn't learn of kaolin from them."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.5800442751","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-24","passage":"<p>Dung beetles roll balls of dung away from crowded piles in straight lines, which helps them escape competitors. To test how the beetles maintain their course at night, biologist Marie Dacke placed small caps over the beetles' heads that blocked their view of the sky. Capped beetles wandered in circles, while beetles wearing clear caps rolled straight. On moonless nights, uncapped beetles still rolled straight, but when tested in a planetarium where only the Milky Way was projected, they also rolled straight. Dacke concluded that the beetles ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["rely primarily on the moon to navigate and become disoriented on moonless nights.","roll their dung balls in circles to confuse competitors in the dark.","navigate by sensing the slope and texture of the ground beneath them rather than by looking at the moon or stars in the night sky.","can use the faint glow of the Milky Way to keep their course when the moon is not visible."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Blocking the sky made beetles circle, so they use the sky. They still steered on moonless nights and under a projected Milky Way alone, so they can use the Milky Way. They did not get lost without the moon. Circling was the result of being capped, not a tactic. And capped beetles circled, which rules out steering by the ground."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.758799d4d3","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-25","passage":"<p>Economists have observed that when a city opens a new highway lane to relieve traffic congestion, traffic often returns to its previous levels within a few years. Some analysts attribute this to population growth. But a study of U.S. metropolitan areas found that vehicle miles traveled rose roughly in proportion to new road capacity even in areas where population was stable. The finding implies that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["cities with stable populations experience less traffic congestion than growing cities do.","adding road capacity may itself encourage additional driving, regardless of changes in population.","population growth is the main reason that new highway lanes fail to reduce congestion in most U.S. metropolitan areas.","adding lanes to highways permanently reduces traffic congestion in most metropolitan areas."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Driving rose with new road capacity even where population didn't change, so population growth can't be the full explanation; capacity itself seems to bring more driving. That contradicts the population explanation. The text doesn't compare congestion between stable and growing cities, and it says congestion returns, so the relief isn't permanent."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.c0db70e750","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-26","passage":"<p>The Maya city of Tikal supported tens of thousands of people for centuries before it was largely abandoned around 900 CE. Analysis of sediment from the city's reservoirs shows that by the 800s, two of its largest reservoirs contained toxic levels of mercury, much of it likely from cinnabar, a red mineral the Maya used in plaster, pottery, and burials, as well as high concentrations of algae that produce harmful toxins. Because the city had few other reliable sources of water during dry seasons, the researchers propose that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["residents of Tikal relied mainly on rivers rather than reservoirs for drinking water during dry seasons.","Tikal was abandoned because its population grew too large for its reservoirs to supply.","the Maya stopped using cinnabar in plaster and pottery once they recognized that it was contaminating their reservoirs with mercury.","polluted reservoirs may have helped drive residents from the city."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Toxic reservoirs plus few other dry-season water sources could have made the city hard to live in, which fits the abandonment. The text doesn't say the Maya recognized the problem or stopped using cinnabar. Overpopulation isn't discussed. The text says the city had few other reliable water sources, so rivers weren't the main supply."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.2cceb1675c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-27","passage":"<p>Linguist Lera Boroditsky asked speakers of Spanish and German to describe a bridge. In German, the word for bridge is grammatically feminine; in Spanish, it is masculine. German speakers more often used words such as \"elegant\" and \"slender,\" while Spanish speakers more often chose \"strong\" and \"sturdy.\" The pattern reversed for a key, which is masculine in German and feminine in Spanish. All participants completed the task in English. If these results are accurate, they suggest that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["a noun's grammatical gender in one's native language can shape how one thinks about the object.","German speakers generally prefer objects that are elegant and slender, while Spanish speakers prefer objects that are strong and sturdy, regardless of the language they are using.","words describing objects in English carry grammatical gender that affects speakers' descriptions.","speakers of German and Spanish have difficulty describing objects accurately in English."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Descriptions followed the noun's gender in the speaker's native language, even though everyone worked in English. That suggests native grammar shapes thinking. Nothing shows inaccurate English. English nouns don't have grammatical gender. The reversal for \"key\" rules out a simple preference for elegant or sturdy things."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.674e52e405","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-28","passage":"<p>Historians have long cited the diary of Samuel Pepys as evidence of how Londoners experienced the Great Fire of 1666. But Pepys was a well-connected naval official who could move his valuables by boat and who dined with members of the court during the crisis. Records of the fire's poorer victims, by contrast, survive mainly in petitions for charity filed months later, which describe lost tools, rented rooms, and months of homelessness. Taken together, these sources suggest that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["Pepys's experience of the fire may have been unusually comfortable.","poorer Londoners suffered less from the fire than historians have assumed.","Pepys exaggerated the damage caused by the fire in his diary in order to gain favor with the members of the court with whom he dined.","petitions for charity are a more reliable source than diaries because they were written later."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Pepys had resources, like a boat and court connections, that poorer victims lacked, and their petitions describe hardship. So his account may not represent most Londoners' experience. There's no evidence that he exaggerated. The petitions suggest more suffering, not less, and being written later doesn't make a source more reliable."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.aa0f208985","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-29","passage":"<p>Plants of the species Arabidopsis thaliana normally flower in spring after experiencing a prolonged period of cold. Biologist Caroline Dean found that exposure to cold gradually switches off a gene called FLC, which blocks flowering. The longer the cold lasts, the more cells in the plant shut the gene down, and the gene stays off even after temperatures rise. A brief cold snap in autumn therefore does not cause the plant to flower, because ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the gene FLC encourages flowering once temperatures begin to rise.","plants that experience cold in autumn permanently lose the ability to flower.","too few cells will have shut down FLC.","FLC is activated only by the warm temperatures that return after a brief cold snap in autumn."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gene turns off gradually, with more cells shutting it down as cold lasts longer. A short cold snap won't silence enough cells, so the flowering block remains. The text doesn't describe what activates FLC, nothing says autumn cold permanently prevents flowering, and FLC blocks flowering rather than encouraging it."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.c8f5fa0455","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-30","passage":"<p>Studies show that people tend to rate a wine as better tasting when they are told it is expensive. In one experiment, researchers scanned participants' brains while they drank the same wine labeled with different prices. Activity increased in a brain region associated with the experience of pleasure when participants believed the wine cost more, even though the wine was identical. This finding suggests that price labels ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["lead people to misreport their preferences so that they appear more sophisticated to the researchers conducting the study.","accurately reflect differences in the quality of wines.","may alter how much people actually enjoy a product, not merely what they say about it.","have no effect on people who are experienced wine drinkers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Brain activity tied to pleasure rose with the belief in a higher price, so the label seems to change the experience itself, not just what people report. That argues against the idea that people are only pretending. Experienced drinkers aren't discussed, and since the wine was identical, the prices didn't reflect quality."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.f9f6d85ada","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-31","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1917 short story. Mrs. Bayliss has invited her new neighbor, Mrs. Rourke, for tea.</p><p>Mrs. Bayliss had brought out the good cups, the ones with the gold rims that had been her mother's, and she had spent the morning on a lemon cake. Mrs. Rourke admired the cups, ate two slices of cake, and talked pleasantly for an hour about her children and the price of coal. It was only after she had gone, and Mrs. Bayliss was rinsing the cups, that she realized her guest had not asked her a single question. The text suggests that Mrs. Bayliss ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["regrets using her mother's gold-rimmed cups for a guest who did not appreciate them.","believes that Mrs. Rourke was deliberately rude to her during the visit.","had hoped for some interest in herself.","was relieved that Mrs. Rourke did not ask about her personal life."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Mrs. Bayliss prepared with care and only afterward notices that Mrs. Rourke asked her nothing. That realization implies she had wanted some interest in return. Mrs. Rourke did admire the cups. Nothing suggests Mrs. Bayliss thinks the rudeness was deliberate, and the belated realization suggests disappointment, not relief."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.325a868b8c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-32","passage":"<p>For decades, the standard treatment for peanut allergy in children was strict avoidance. In a trial published in 2015, researchers randomly assigned infants at high risk of the allergy either to avoid peanuts or to eat small amounts of peanut products regularly until age five. About 17 percent of the avoidance group developed a peanut allergy, compared with about 3 percent of the group that ate peanut products. The researchers concluded that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["strict avoidance of peanuts is the most effective way to prevent peanut allergy in infants.","peanut allergies are less common among children than researchers had previously believed.","children who develop a peanut allergy before age five usually outgrow it by the time they reach adulthood.","early, regular exposure to peanuts may help prevent the allergy."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Infants who regularly ate peanut products developed the allergy far less often (3 versus 17 percent) than those who avoided peanuts, so early exposure may prevent it. That reverses the old avoidance advice. The trial says nothing about outgrowing the allergy or about how common it is overall."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.352eda11d0","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-33","passage":"<p>Honeybees communicate the location of food by dancing, and the angle of the dance indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun. Researchers have noticed that bees that dance long after they return to the hive gradually shift the angle of their dance over time, even without seeing the sky. The shift closely matches the movement of the sun across the sky during the same period. This observation suggests that bees ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["can detect the sun's position through the walls of the hive by sensing changes in temperature.","dance only when the sun is directly above the hive.","rely on smell rather than direction to guide other bees to food.","can track the sun's movement with an internal clock."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The dance angle shifts to match the sun's movement even though the bees can't see the sky. That points to an internal clock that tracks where the sun would be. The observation specifies that the bees can't see the sky. Smell isn't mentioned, and bees keep dancing and adjusting over time, not only at noon."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.97c61e2c0a","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-34","passage":"<p>Archaeologists excavating a Viking Age settlement in Iceland found that the earliest houses were built with large amounts of birch wood, while houses built a century later used mostly turf and imported driftwood. Pollen records from nearby lake sediments show that birch pollen declined sharply during the same period, while pollen from grasses increased. Taken together, this evidence suggests that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["birch trees in Iceland were destroyed by a sudden change in climate shortly before the first Viking settlers arrived on the island.","the Viking settlers preferred turf to birch wood because turf provided better insulation.","settlers depleted the local birch woodland.","driftwood was more plentiful in Iceland during the first century of settlement than it was later."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Birch use fell as birch pollen declined and grasses spread, which fits the settlers depleting the woodland and turning to turf and driftwood. Insulation preferences aren't discussed. The birch decline happened during settlement, not before it. And later builders used more driftwood, which suggests it didn't become scarcer."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.21a8a16f56","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-35","passage":"<p>When the state of Oregon expanded its Medicaid program in 2008, it had funding for only some of the eligible applicants and chose recipients by lottery. Economists compared lottery winners with applicants who were not selected. Within two years, winners used more health care services, reported better mental health, and were far less likely to face catastrophic medical bills. However, measures of blood pressure and cholesterol did not differ significantly between the groups. Based on these findings, a researcher would be most justified in concluding that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["lottery winners had healthier blood pressure and cholesterol levels than applicants who were not selected.","gaining insurance had no meaningful effect on the well-being of participants in the lottery.","insurance improved some aspects of well-being, but no effect on certain physical measures was detected.","the lottery was an unfair way to choose recipients because it excluded many eligible applicants who would have used more health care services and faced fewer catastrophic medical bills."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Winners did better on care use, mental health, and financial protection, but no physical-measure difference was detected over two years. The correct choice captures both parts. \"No meaningful effect\" ignores the positive results. The groups did not differ on blood pressure and cholesterol. And the text doesn't judge whether the lottery was fair."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.36309f6312","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-36","passage":"<p>The novelist Sarah Orne Jewett is often described as a \"regionalist,\" a writer concerned mainly with the customs and speech of rural Maine. Critic Marjorie Pryse notes that many of Jewett's stories center on women who live without husbands and who support themselves as healers, farmers, or boardinghouse keepers, lives that were rarely treated as the subjects of serious fiction in the 1880s. Pryse argues that the label \"regionalist\" has encouraged readers to see Jewett's focus on these women as local color rather than as ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["an accurate description of the customs and speech of rural Maine.","a sign that Jewett's stories about healers, farmers, and boardinghouse keepers were intended mainly for readers in rural Maine.","evidence that Jewett was unfamiliar with the literary trends of her time.","a challenge to ideas about whose lives merited serious fiction."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Pryse says Jewett centered independent women whose lives serious fiction usually ignored, and that the \"regionalist\" label hides this. The missing phrase names what readers overlook: a deliberate challenge. Describing Maine customs is exactly the \"local color\" reading that Pryse says the label encourages. Unfamiliarity with trends and a Maine-only audience aren't supported."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.f7194b7477","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-37","passage":"<p>Crows in some Japanese cities have been observed placing walnuts on roads so that passing cars crack them open. Researchers noticed that the crows most often drop nuts at intersections with traffic lights, and that they retrieve the nut meat when the light turns red and cars stop. Crows in rural areas with few intersections rarely show this behavior, even when walnut trees are plentiful. These observations suggest that the behavior ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["depends on features of city streets.","developed because rural crows lack the strength to crack walnuts on their own.","is an instinct shared by all crows that have access to walnut trees, whether they live in cities or in rural areas.","causes traffic accidents at intersections where crows gather."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Crows use lights to retrieve nuts safely, and rural crows rarely do it despite plenty of walnuts. So the behavior depends on city features. If it were a shared instinct, rural crows would do it too. The strength of rural crows isn't discussed, and nothing is said about accidents."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.d2db09a65c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-38","passage":"<p>In the nineteenth century, many European scholars assumed that the great stone ruins of Great Zimbabwe could not have been built by the ancestors of the local Shona people and credited them instead to foreign visitors. Archaeological work in the twentieth century found that the site's pottery, metalwork, and building techniques closely matched those of other Shona settlements, and radiocarbon dating placed its construction between the 1100s and 1400s. These findings indicate that the nineteenth-century assumptions were most likely based on ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["the discovery of foreign building techniques in the great stone ruins at Great Zimbabwe.","accurate radiocarbon dates obtained by European scholars.","assumptions about the local people rather than evidence.","careful analyses of the pottery and metalwork found at Great Zimbabwe."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Evidence later showed the site matched Shona settlements, so the earlier attribution to foreigners wasn't based on site evidence. That points to prejudgment. Pottery analysis and radiocarbon dating came in the twentieth century and pointed the other way. The building techniques matched local ones, not foreign ones."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.36c9def43b","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-39","passage":"<p>Many species of songbirds learn their songs by listening to adult males during a critical period early in life. Zebra finches raised in isolation produce abnormal songs. In one experiment, researchers raised isolated finches and then allowed them to form a colony over several generations. Each generation learned from the previous one, and within three or four generations, the colony's songs had come to closely resemble those of wild zebra finches. The experiment suggests that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["zebra finches raised in isolation are unable to learn any songs from other birds.","the songs of wild zebra finches have changed significantly over the past several generations, becoming more similar to the songs of birds raised in isolation.","normal song features can re-emerge as generations learn from one another.","young zebra finches learn songs more quickly from isolated birds than from wild birds."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Starting from abnormal songs, later generations learning from each other drifted toward wild-type songs without ever hearing wild birds. That shows normal features re-emerging through learning across generations. Isolated birds' offspring did learn songs. Nothing is said about changes in wild songs, and learning speed isn't compared."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.bf0a07c5b9","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-40","passage":"<p>Many online retailers display the number of other customers who are viewing a product at the same moment (\"12 people are looking at this\"). In a study, researchers showed the message to some shoppers and not others. Shoppers who saw the message were more likely to purchase items, but the effect disappeared for products with many available units in stock, where the message was paired with a note that supply was plentiful. The results suggest that the messages increase purchases mainly by ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["providing shoppers with reliable information about a product's quality and popularity.","helping shoppers find products more quickly on a retailer's website.","encouraging shoppers to compare products with those of other retailers.","suggesting an item may soon run out."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The effect disappeared once shoppers learned that supply was plentiful, so the message works through a sense of scarcity. If it signaled quality, the stock note shouldn't erase the effect. Search speed and price comparison aren't mentioned."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.08ce2b9703","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data2-41","passage":"<p>The poet Emily Dickinson published fewer than a dozen poems during her lifetime, and those that appeared were heavily edited: editors replaced her dashes with commas and periods and altered her unusual rhymes. When scholar Thomas Johnson published an edition in 1955 that restored her original punctuation, some readers found the poems strange and difficult. Others argued that the dashes create pauses and ambiguities essential to the poems' meaning. For the second group of readers, the earlier edited versions most likely seemed ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["to have lost something essential when made conventional.","more difficult to understand than the restored versions.","to have been published without Dickinson's knowledge.","more faithful to Dickinson's intentions than Johnson's edition, which restored her original punctuation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Readers who think the dashes are essential would see the edited versions, with dashes removed, as missing something vital. They would not think those versions were more faithful. They'd find the restored versions richer, not the edited ones harder. And nothing in the text concerns whether Dickinson knew about the publications."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.39d6ae7c7d","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-52","passage":"<p>A common explanation for why yawning is contagious holds that it spreads empathy among members of a group. Researchers testing this idea measured how often participants yawned in response to videos of strangers yawning and compared the results with participants' scores on standard empathy questionnaires. Across several hundred participants, contagious yawning showed no relationship to empathy scores but declined steadily with age and varied with time of day and tiredness. These findings suggest that contagious yawning ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["has no function in humans or in other social animals.","may be driven by physiological factors rather than by social ones.","is unrelated to how tired a person is at the time of testing.","occurs more often among people with high scores on standard empathy questionnaires than among those with low scores."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Yawning tracked age, time of day, and tiredness but not empathy, which points to physiology rather than social connection. The empathy link is what the data rule out, tiredness did matter, and finding no empathy link doesn't show yawning has no function at all."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.cd670623bf","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-53","passage":"<p>Archaeologists excavating a 9,000-year-old settlement found that houses were rebuilt repeatedly on exactly the same footprint, with new walls raised directly on the rubble of old ones. Over centuries this produced a mound of stacked houses. Rebuilding on level ground nearby would have been easier, and the settlement had ample space. The archaeologists therefore propose that the practice ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["reflected a deliberate attachment to a particular house's location rather than a practical constraint.","allowed residents to build new houses more quickly than starting again on the level ground nearby would have.","was necessary because the settlement had little unused land available for new construction.","indicates that the residents were unaware that nearby ground was level and unoccupied."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Rebuilding in place was harder and space was available, so practical explanations fall away, leaving a deliberate attachment to the spot. Speed and lack of land both contradict the text, and residents who lived there for centuries would know the nearby ground."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.dc48c6c64f","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-54","passage":"<p>Two groups of students were given the same passage to study. One group reread it three times; the other read it once and then wrote down everything they could recall. Immediately afterward, the rereading group rated their own mastery higher and scored slightly higher on a quiz. A week later, the recall group scored substantially higher, and their confidence ratings had been much closer to their eventual performance. These results suggest that rereading ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["can create a feeling of mastery that fades faster than the learning it seems to indicate.","produces better retention a week later than writing down everything one can recall does.","makes students less confident about material than practicing recall does.","is the most efficient way to prepare for a quiz given immediately after studying."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Rereaders felt more masterful and did slightly better right away but much worse a week later, so their confidence outran their durable learning. They retained less over time, and they were more confident, not less. The immediate-quiz claim overstates a slight edge and ignores the point about the illusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.7eaf2a473e","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-55","passage":"<p>Ravens are known to hide food and to watch other ravens hide food. In one experiment, a raven that had been able to observe a hidden cache through a peephole later retrieved food faster when it believed an observer might have watched it hide its own cache, even when no other raven was present and only the sound of a raven was played. Researchers concluded that the raven's behavior depended on ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["its memory of the places where other ravens had hidden food on previous occasions.","its ability to recognize individual ravens by their calls.","the presence of a visible competitor near the cache site.","its inference that it might be watched, rather than on the sight of another raven."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The raven hurried when it could infer that an unseen observer might be present, with no visible bird, so the trigger was the inference itself. The experiment involved the raven's own cache rather than memories of others' caches, no competitor was visible, and nothing tested recognizing individuals."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.f1ff4c64c6","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-56","passage":"<p>Economists studying a national program that gave families cash transfers expected spending on alcohol and tobacco to rise, an objection often raised against such programs. Instead, across nineteen studies in several countries, spending on those goods fell slightly in most cases. One proposed explanation notes that families receiving transfers also reported sharp declines in stress about food. If this explanation is correct, then the drop in spending on alcohol and tobacco most likely occurred because ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["relief from financial stress reduced the reasons families had been buying those goods.","the price of alcohol and tobacco rose during the period the transfers were made.","families receiving the cash transfers were required by the program to avoid purchasing alcohol and tobacco.","families in the studies spent nearly all of the transfers on food rather than on other goods."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The proposed explanation links falling stress to falling purchases, so less stress removed a reason to buy. The text gives no program restriction, mentions no price change, and doesn't claim every dollar went to food."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.93f87be78c","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-57","passage":"<p>Manuscripts copied in medieval scriptoria often contain identical errors, which scholars use to reconstruct which copies descend from which. A newly examined twelfth-century manuscript shares several distinctive errors with a manuscript in Vienna but also contains passages missing from the Vienna copy that appear in an older manuscript in Cairo. The simplest explanation for this pattern is that the new manuscript's scribe ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["made more errors than the scribes of the Vienna and Cairo manuscripts.","copied the Vienna manuscript without consulting any other source.","worked from more than one exemplar while copying.","invented the passages that appear in the Cairo manuscript but not in the Vienna manuscript."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shared errors point to the Vienna line, while passages found only in the Cairo copy must come from elsewhere, so the scribe used more than one source. Copying only Vienna can't explain the extra passages, invention is a less simple explanation than a second exemplar, and error counts aren't compared."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.6987db4182","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-58","passage":"<p>Public health officials in a coastal city noticed that emergency room visits for asthma rose on days when ozone levels were high. They also found that ozone levels were highest on hot, sunny days, when more residents spent time outdoors. A researcher reviewing the data cautioned that the correlation between ozone and asthma visits may be partly explained by the fact that ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["emergency rooms are more likely to be open on hot, sunny days than on cool days.","ozone levels in the city are measured less accurately on hot, sunny days than they are on cooler days.","asthma is more common among residents of coastal cities than among other residents.","people breathe more outdoor air on the days when ozone levels happen to be highest."],"answer":"D","explanation":"On hot, sunny days people are outside more, so exposure rises along with ozone, which could inflate the apparent link. Nothing suggests measurement problems, city-to-city asthma rates don't explain day-to-day variation, and emergency rooms don't close on cool days."},
{"id":"g.rw.inferences.3.c9387a7c04","section":"rw","domain":"info","skill":"inferences","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-IN","group":"hard-hard_data7-59","passage":"<p>When a new highway bypass opened around the town of Halvard, business owners on the old main street predicted ruin. Sales at gas stations and fast-food outlets along the old route did fall sharply. Sales at the town's restaurants, hardware store, and bookshop, however, rose over the following three years, and several new businesses opened on streets that had carried heavy through traffic. This pattern suggests that the bypass ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["caused most of the town's residents to shop in other towns instead.","reduced the total amount of business activity in the town of Halvard over the three years that followed its opening.","hurt businesses that depended on passing drivers while benefiting those serving residents.","was opposed by the owners of the town's restaurants, hardware store, and bookshop."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Businesses serving drivers lost sales while those serving residents grew and new ones opened, which splits the effect by customer type. Total activity rose in several sectors, the text doesn't identify who opposed the bypass, and residents evidently shopped locally."}
]);
